From d87880ed239f9bf79076b7d23377a1993e8f7d45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vulps22 <ajmcallister5@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 03:24:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] secur32: Support resuming a parked handshake after
 SEC_I_INCOMPLETE_CREDENTIALS.

GnuTLS's gnutls_handshake() runs synchronously to completion in one
call, with no way to pause mid-handshake and resume once new
credentials arrive. Real Windows SChannel's SEC_I_INCOMPLETE_CREDENTIALS
retry contract requires exactly that: the app gets told to reacquire
credentials and retry with the same context handle.

Bridges the two by running the handshake on a worker thread. When
GnuTLS's certificate-retrieve callback (cert_retrieve_cb) is invoked
with no certificate available yet, the worker parks there instead of
letting GnuTLS send an empty Certificate and complete the handshake.
schan_update_session_creds() wakes it once schannel.c's
establish_context() supplies a real credential via the documented
retry path (a new phCredential on an existing phContext).

Two more things this needed:

- The retry call's input token is an empty placeholder (SSPI docs:
  "call again like the first time, without any input"), which
  establish_context() would otherwise treat as an incomplete network
  read and reject before ever reaching the handshake call.

- Unconsumed bytes of the server's TLS flight at the moment the
  handshake parks (in practice the trailing ServerHelloDone record)
  live in a SecBufferDesc that doesn't outlive the call that parked
  us. They're copied out and drained by pull_adapter() on resume, or
  the resumed handshake reads nothing, gets EAGAIN, and both ends
  wait for each other.

schan_create_session() only wires this up for a client session that
starts out with no certificate at all; every other session is
unaffected.
